+### Writing `rejection_description`
+It is the technical record of the rejection for the operators who check the
+decision: the logic, and every fact behind it. Write it from the approved
+investigation, in this order:
+
+1. The rule, check or module that rejected the packet, and what it requires.
+2. How this packet met it: each condition, with the value that decided it.
+3. Every packet-specific fact the investigation reports, including each line
+   of its KEY FACTS list: every identifier involved -- each candidate or
+   matched record, the parent or master record it belongs to, and their EIDs
+   and UIDs -- and each classification, match result, score, threshold, count
+   and flag, with its value.
+
+Copy every identifier and value exactly as the investigation gives it, in
+full; mask or withhold none of them. Never shorten an identifier to its first
+characters, round a score, or replace a list with "several" or "one or more".
+When several records are involved, name each one with its own values. Leave
+out any fact the investigation reports as not in the logs.
+
+Leave out what does not explain the decision: timestamps (unless a condition
+depends on one), pod and topic names, what the service published or logged
+after the decision, and how complete the logs were.
+
+### Writing `synthesis`
+It is the summary of the case in simple English, at most 3 sentences: what
+the resident tried to do, where and why the packet failed, and the resolution
+the SERVICE POLICY gives for this case, without alternatives it does not give.
+It may give the details that matter, such as what matched and the deciding
+values, but no personal data: leave out EIDs, UIDs, VIDs, names, dates of
+birth, contact details and addresses.
